  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis this afternoon depict upper \nridging over the Central Plains as the mid-level ridge begins to \nbreak down. The remnants of a few showers and weak thunderstorms \nfrom earlier today continue to track into eastern Kansas, and most \nof the forecast area is expected to remain dry through the remainder \nof the day.\n\nThe latest short-term guidance continues to highlight a deepening \nshortwave tracking over the Northern/Central Plains later today into \nFriday morning. Showers and storms developing ahead of the \nassociated cold front will move through a majority of the region \nlater tonight into Friday morning amid an anomalously-moist airmass \n(PWs well above 2 inches). Per the prior discussion, strong to \nsevere wind gusts are possible considering the abnormal moisture in \nplace and DCAPE values in excess of 1000 J/kg, though these should \nremain isolated owing to sufficient convective inhibition at the \nsurface. Activity will likely remain organized into the morning \nowing to support from the LLJ, but better organization to this point \nmay be further to our north and east where the main nose of the jet \nwill reside. Rain chances should come to an end by mid morning as \nforcing shifts to the south and east. In the wake of the cold front, \nlocations in central Kansas will see seasonable highs in the low \n90s, while portions of southern and especially southeast Kansas may \nsee highs in the middle to upper 90s depending on how quickly the \nfront and associated \"cooler\" air arrive.\n\nMid/upper ridging will continue across the Desert Southwest \nalongside broad troughing across the eastern CONUS as we head into \nthe weekend. This pattern will look to keep the forecast area \nslightly cooler this weekend with northerly flow aloft and at the \nsurface throughout the region. As such, highs mostly in the 80s are \nforecast on Saturday along with lows in the low to middle 60s \nheading into Sunday. This cooldown will be short-lived, however, as \nheights and thicknesses begin to increase with the eastward shift of \nthe mid/upper ridge. As a result, a warming trend is expected \nto take place through the work week with widespread highs in the\nmiddle to upper 90s by Tuesday/Wednesday.",
